        "topic": "Gas Laws",
        "question_type": "Objective",
        "exam_year": "2001",
        "question_text": "<p>The volume of a given mass of gas is 200 cm<sup>3</sup> at 27\u00b0C. What will be its volume at 54\u00b0C if the pressure remains constant?</p>",
        "question_image": null,
        "answer_image": null,
        "explanation": "<p>Using Charles's Law, which states that $V_1/T_1 = V_2/T_2$, where temperatures must be in Kelvin. Converting temperatures: $T_1 = 27\u00b0C + 273 = 300 K$, $T_2 = 54\u00b0C + 273 = 327 K$.<br>Thus, $V_2 = (V_1 \times T_2) / T_1 = (200 \times 327) / 300 = 218 \text{ cm}^3$.</p>",
        "reference_passage": null,
        "novel": null,
        "comments_count": 0,
        "options": [
            {
                "option_id": "82683",
